---
title: Execution context
description: The ErgosfareContext every participant receives — cancellation, per-dispatch state, aborting, and why it is pooled.
---

Every handler and interceptor receives an `ErgosfareContext`. It carries the cancellation token,
a bag of per-dispatch state, and the way to stop a pipeline.

```csharp
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    context.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    …
}
```

## Cancellation

`context.CancellationToken` is the token the dispatch was started with. Pass it down rather
than reaching for `CancellationToken.None`:

```csharp
await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
```

Every mediator call takes a token, and the ones that do not take one explicitly default it:

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command, cancellationToken);
```

## Per-dispatch state

`Items` is a dictionary shared by every participant in one dispatch. It is how a pre-interceptor
tells a later stage something without changing the message:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("startedAt", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("startedAt", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

The accessors:

| Member | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Set(key, value)` | Stores a value. |
| `Has(key)` | Whether a key is present. |
| `Get<T>(key)` | Reads it, throwing when absent or the wrong type. |
| `TryGet<T>(key, out value)` | Reads it without throwing. |
| `Items` | The underlying dictionary, for anything the above does not cover. |

{% aside type="caution" %}
`Items` is per dispatch, not per request. A nested dispatch gets its own context unless you
pass yours explicitly, so it is the wrong place for something an HTTP request scope should own.
Put that in a scoped service and inject it.
{% /aside %}

## Stopping the pipeline

`Abort()` ends the dispatch immediately:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (_tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("no tenant on this request");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

It throws `ExecutionAbortedException`, and the pipeline treats that as a stop rather than a
failure: **the exception stage does not run, and neither does the final stage.** The signal
travels to the caller unchanged.

That distinction is the whole point of having a separate signal. A failure is something that
went wrong and that a final interceptor should still observe; an abort is a decision that this
dispatch should not continue.

Overloads carry a reason and a value:

```csharp
context.Abort("not authorized");
context.Abort("quota exceeded", remainingQuota);
```

## Passing a context along

Every dispatch surface takes an optional context. Passing yours makes the nested dispatch share
your `Items` and your cancellation:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(command.Id), context);
}
```

Omitting it gives the nested dispatch a fresh context instead. Which you want depends on whether
the inner work is part of the same logical operation — see
[Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

## Scopes

`CreateScope()` gives a nested lifetime within one dispatch, for state that should not outlive
a section of the pipeline:

```csharp
using var scope = context.CreateScope();
```

## Why it is pooled

Contexts are pooled and returned when the dispatch completes. That is what keeps a dispatch from
allocating one per call on the hot path, and it has one consequence worth knowing:

{% aside type="danger" %}
**Do not keep a context after your participant returns.** Storing it in a field, closing over it
in a background task, or handing it to something that outlives the dispatch means you are
holding an object that has already been returned to the pool and may now belong to an unrelated
dispatch. Copy the values you need instead.
{% /aside %}

```csharp
// wrong — the context is gone by the time this runs
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(context.Get<string>("user")));

// right — read it first
var user = context.Get<string>("user");
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(user));
```

The pooling is also why there is no `AsyncLocal` anywhere in the dispatch path: the context is a
parameter, so it flows exactly as far as you pass it and no further.
